<!--
  Skeletal (fill-in) version of 7.3 Hyperbolic Functions.

  Definitions, identities, and problem statements are kept; derivations,
  solutions, and finished graphs are replaced by blank workspace boxes
  and empty grids, so the notes can be completed by hand (e.g. on an
  iPad over the PDF, or printed).  The real-life applications are kept
  in full.  Content mirrors ch-7-3.ptx; every xml:id here is prefixed
  with "skel-" so the two chapters can coexist in the same book.
-->
<chapter xml:id="skel-chapter-hyperbolic-functions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Hyperbolic Functions (Skeletal Notes)</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Define the hyperbolic sine and cosine in terms of <m>e^x</m> and
<m>e^{-x}</m>, and differentiate them.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Sketch the graphs of <m>\sinh x</m> and <m>\cosh x</m>, and state their
domains and ranges.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Use the identity <m>\cosh^2 x - \sinh^2 x = 1</m> and relate it to the
parametrization of the hyperbola <m>x^2 - y^2 = 1</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Work with the remaining hyperbolic functions <m>\tanh x</m>,
<m>\coth x</m>, <m>\operatorname{sech} x</m>, and
<m>\operatorname{csch} x</m>, and apply hyperbolic identities to
integrals and equations.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<introduction>
<p>
This chapter is about certain combinations of <m>e^x</m> and
<m>e^{-x}</m> which appear in multiple branches of science and
engineering. In many ways they are analogous to the trigonometric
functions, and they have a similar relationship to the hyperbola that
the trigonometric functions have to the circle, which is why they are
known as <term>hyperbolic functions</term>. We will study these
relationships gradually during this chapter.
</p>
<p>
<em>These are skeletal notes: the definitions, identities, and problem
statements are given, and blank boxes and empty grids are left wherever
a derivation, a solution, or a sketch belongs, so that you can fill
them in by hand.</em>
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-hyp-definitions">
<title>Definitions and Derivatives</title>

<p>
The <term>hyperbolic cosine</term> is defined as

<md xml:id="eq-skel-hyp-cosh-def" number="yes">
\cosh x = \frac{e^x + e^{-x}}{2},
</md>
</p>

<p>
and the <term>hyperbolic sine</term> is defined as

<md xml:id="eq-skel-hyp-sinh-def" number="yes">
\sinh x = \frac{e^x - e^{-x}}{2}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Find the derivatives <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}(\cosh x)</m> and
<m>\dfrac{d}{dx}(\sinh x)</m> by differentiating the two definitions.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,4.25) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for a handwritten derivation.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</section>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-hyp-graphs">
<title>Graph, Domain, and Range of <m>\cosh x</m> and <m>\sinh x</m></title>

<p>
Since <m>\sinh x</m> and <m>\cosh x</m> are built from the two
exponentials <m>\tfrac12 e^x</m> and <m>\tfrac12 e^{-x}</m>, their
graphs are easiest to understand by sketching those exponentials first.
See <xref ref="fig-skel-hyp-sinh-graph"/> and
<xref ref="fig-skel-hyp-cosh-graph"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-hyp-sinh-graph">
<caption>
The graph of <m>y = \sinh x</m>. The curve is squeezed between the two
exponentials <m>y = \tfrac12 e^x</m> and <m>y = -\tfrac12 e^{-x}</m>,
approaching the first as <m>x \to \infty</m> and the second as
<m>x \to -\infty</m>.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-hyp-sinh">
<diagram dimensions="(360,300)" margins="12">
<definition>s(t) = (t, (exp(t) - exp(-t))/2)</definition>
<definition>ep(t) = (t, exp(t)/2)</definition>
<definition>em(t) = (t, -exp(-t)/2)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.6,-3.4,2.6,3.4)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="sh-ep" function="ep" domain="(-2.5,1.85)"
                  stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="sh-em" function="em" domain="(-1.85,2.5)"
                  stroke="#E69F00" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="sh-s" function="s" domain="(-1.92,1.92)"
                  stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="3"/>
<label at="sh-s-lab" anchor="(1.25,2.35)" alignment="w" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>y=\sinh x</m>
</label>
<label at="sh-ep-lab" anchor="(-2.4,0.55)" alignment="ne" color="#56B4E9">
<m>y=\frac12 e^{x}</m>
</label>
<label at="sh-em-lab" anchor="(1.05,-0.85)" alignment="se" color="#E69F00">
<m>y=-\frac12 e^{-x}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Three curves on a coordinate grid. A dark red curve, y equals hyperbolic sine of x, rises from the lower left through the origin to the upper right. A light blue curve, y equals one half e to the x, lies just above it on the right, and an orange curve, y equals negative one half e to the negative x, lies just below it on the left.">
  <annotation ref="sh-s" text="The graph of hyperbolic sine: an increasing curve through the origin, odd about the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="sh-ep" text="The exponential one half e to the x, which hyperbolic sine approaches as x grows."/>
  <annotation ref="sh-em" text="The exponential negative one half e to the negative x, which hyperbolic sine approaches as x decreases."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
From the graph, read off the domain, the range, and the symmetry of
<m>\sinh x</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,5.4);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.6) -- (15,3.6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,1.8) -- (15,1.8);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.25,5.15) {Domain:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.25,3.35) {Range:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.25,1.55) {Symmetry:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box with rows labelled Domain, Range, and Symmetry, for observations about hyperbolic sine.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
The graph suggests that <m>\sinh x</m> hugs <m>\tfrac12 e^x</m> when
<m>x</m> is large and positive, and hugs <m>-\tfrac12 e^{-x}</m> when
<m>x</m> is large and negative. Confirm this observation.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,5.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for confirming the limiting behaviour of hyperbolic sine by hand.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-hyp-cosh-graph">
<caption>
The graph of <m>y = \cosh x</m>. The curve is the sum of the two
exponentials <m>y = \tfrac12 e^x</m> and <m>y = \tfrac12 e^{-x}</m>, so
it lies above both and has its minimum value <m>1</m> at <m>x = 0</m>.
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-hyp-cosh">
<diagram dimensions="(360,300)" margins="12">
<definition>c(t) = (t, (exp(t) + exp(-t))/2)</definition>
<definition>ep(t) = (t, exp(t)/2)</definition>
<definition>em(t) = (t, exp(-t)/2)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.6,-0.8,2.6,4.0)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="ch-ep" function="ep" domain="(-2.5,2.05)"
                  stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ch-em" function="em" domain="(-2.05,2.5)"
                  stroke="#E69F00" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="ch-c" function="c" domain="(-2.0,2.0)"
                  stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="3"/>
<point at="ch-min" p="(0,1)" fill="#8B1A4A"/>
<label at="ch-c-lab" anchor="(1.05,3.35)" alignment="w" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>y=\cosh x</m>
</label>
<label at="ch-ep-lab" anchor="(1.30,0.85)" alignment="se" color="#56B4E9">
<m>y=\frac12 e^{x}</m>
</label>
<label at="ch-em-lab" anchor="(-1.30,0.85)" alignment="sw" color="#E69F00">
<m>y=\frac12 e^{-x}</m>
</label>
<label at="ch-min-lab" anchor="(0.12,1.02)" alignment="se">
<m>1</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Three curves on a coordinate grid. A dark red U shaped curve, y equals hyperbolic cosine of x, has its lowest point at zero comma one. A light blue exponential one half e to the x rises to the right below it, and an orange exponential one half e to the negative x rises to the left below it.">
  <annotation ref="ch-c" text="The graph of hyperbolic cosine: a U shaped curve, even about the y axis, with minimum value one at x equals zero."/>
  <annotation ref="ch-ep" text="The exponential one half e to the x, which hyperbolic cosine approaches as x grows."/>
  <annotation ref="ch-em" text="The exponential one half e to the negative x, which hyperbolic cosine approaches as x decreases."/>
  <annotation ref="ch-min" text="The minimum point zero comma one."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
From the graph, read off the domain, the range, and the symmetry of
<m>\cosh x</m>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,5.4);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.6) -- (15,3.6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,1.8) -- (15,1.8);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.25,5.15) {Domain:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.25,3.35) {Range:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.25,1.55) {Symmetry:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box with rows labelled Domain, Range, and Symmetry, for observations about hyperbolic cosine.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<p>
The graph suggests that <m>\cosh x</m> hugs <m>\tfrac12 e^x</m> when
<m>x</m> is large and positive, and hugs <m>\tfrac12 e^{-x}</m> when
<m>x</m> is large and negative, while never dropping below <m>1</m>.
Confirm these observations.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,5.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for confirming the limiting behaviour of hyperbolic cosine by hand.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</section>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-hyp-identities">
<title>Identities and Other Hyperbolic Functions</title>

<p>
A similar identity to the trigonometric identity
<m>\cos^2 x + \sin^2 x = 1</m> holds for the hyperbolic functions:

<md xml:id="eq-skel-hyp-main-identity" number="yes">
\cosh^2 x - \sinh^2 x = 1.
</md>
</p>

<p>
As you already know, any point <m>P(x,y)</m> on the circumference of
the unit circle <m>(x^2 + y^2 = 1)</m> can be described in terms of sine
and cosine of an angle <m>t \in [0, 2\pi)</m>, i.e. <m>x = \cos t</m>
and <m>y = \sin t</m>, which results in the trigonometric identity
<m>\cos^2 t + \sin^2 t = 1</m>. Mark this on the circle in
<xref ref="fig-skel-hyp-circle-grid"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-hyp-circle-grid">
<caption>
The unit circle <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>. Mark a point
<m>P(\cos t, \sin t)</m> on it, and draw the right triangle with legs
<m>x</m> and <m>y</m> and hypotenuse <m>1</m>.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-hyp-circle-grid">
<diagram dimensions="(320,300)" margins="12">
<definition>circ(t) = (cos(t), sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.7,-1.6,1.7,1.6)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="hc-circle" function="circ" domain="(0,6.2832)"
                  stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="3"/>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The unit circle centered at the origin on a coordinate grid, with nothing else marked, left for labelling by hand: a point P of cosine t comma sine t and the right triangle with legs x and y and hypotenuse one.">
  <annotation ref="hc-circle" text="The unit circle x squared plus y squared equals one."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Similarly, any point <m>P(x,y)</m> on the right branch of the hyperbola
<m>x^2 - y^2 = 1</m> can be represented as <m>x = \cosh t</m> and
<m>y = \sinh t</m>, where <m>t \in \R</m>. This follows directly from
identity <xref ref="eq-skel-hyp-main-identity"/>, i.e.
<m>\cosh^2 t - \sinh^2 t = 1</m>. Mark this on the hyperbola in
<xref ref="fig-skel-hyp-hyperbola-grid"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-hyp-hyperbola-grid">
<caption>
The hyperbola <m>x^2-y^2=1</m>. Mark a point
<m>P(\cosh t, \sinh t)</m> on the right branch, and draw the segments
from the origin to <m>P</m> and to the vertex.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-hyp-hyperbola-grid">
<diagram dimensions="(340,300)" margins="12">
<definition>hr(t) = ((exp(t) + exp(-t))/2, (exp(t) - exp(-t))/2)</definition>
<definition>hl(t) = (-(exp(t) + exp(-t))/2, (exp(t) - exp(-t))/2)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-3.4,-2.9,3.4,2.9)">
<grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
<parametric-curve at="hh-right" function="hr" domain="(-1.65,1.65)"
                  stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="3"/>
<parametric-curve at="hh-left" function="hl" domain="(-1.65,1.65)"
                  stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="3"/>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A hyperbola with two branches opening left and right on a coordinate grid, with nothing else marked, left for labelling by hand: a point P of hyperbolic cosine t comma hyperbolic sine t on the right branch and the segments from the origin.">
  <annotation ref="hh-right" text="The right branch of the hyperbola, traced by the point hyperbolic cosine t comma hyperbolic sine t."/>
  <annotation ref="hh-left" text="The left branch of the hyperbola."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<exercise xml:id="skel-checkpoint-hyp-left-branch">
<title>Parametrizing the Left Branch</title>
<statement>
<p>
Both branches of <m>x^2 - y^2 = 1</m> appear in
<xref ref="fig-skel-hyp-hyperbola-grid"/>, but the parametrization
<m>P(\cosh t, \sinh t)</m> traces only the right one, since
<m>\cosh t \geq 1</m> for every <m>t \in \R</m>. How would you
parametrize the <em>left</em> branch, where <m>x \leq -1</m>?
</p>

<p>
<em>Your answer and reasoning.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,6);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,5.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for a handwritten answer.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
<hint>
<p>
The identity <m>\cosh^2 t - \sinh^2 t = 1</m> is unaffected if you change
the sign of the first coordinate.
</p>
</hint>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="skel-checkpoint-hyp-left-branch-downward">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement>
<p>
The parametrization <m>x = -\cosh t</m>, <m>y = -\sinh t</m>,
<m>t \in \R</m>, also traces the left branch of <m>x^2 - y^2 = 1</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>True or false? Your answer and reasoning.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,4);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,3.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for a handwritten answer.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<p>
As you might have guessed, the rest of the hyperbolic functions are
defined as follows.

<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-skel-hyp-tanh">\tanh x \amp = \frac{\sinh x}{\cosh x}
\amp \operatorname{sech} x \amp = \frac{1}{\cosh x}</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-skel-hyp-coth">\coth x \amp = \frac{\cosh x}{\sinh x}
\amp \operatorname{csch} x \amp = \frac{1}{\sinh x}</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</section>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-hyp-identity-list">
<title>Hyperbolic Identities</title>

<p>
Below are some identities that you may find useful in some problems,
however, you are not expected to memorize them. We will prove some of
them as an exercise later. Each hyperbolic identity is listed next to
the trigonometric identity it resembles.
</p>

<table xml:id="skel-table-hyp-trig-identities">
<title>Hyperbolic identities and their trigonometric counterparts.</title>
<tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
<row header="yes" bottom="major">
<cell>Hyperbolic</cell>
<cell>Trigonometric</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>\cosh^2 x - \sinh^2 x = 1</m></cell>
<cell><m>\cos^2 x + \sin^2 x = 1</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>1 - \tanh^2 x = \operatorname{sech}^2 x</m></cell>
<cell><m>1 + \tan^2 x = \sec^2 x</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>\sinh 2x = 2 \sinh x \cosh x</m></cell>
<cell><m>\sin 2x = 2 \sin x \cos x</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>\cosh 2x = \cosh^2 x + \sinh^2 x</m></cell>
<cell><m>\cos 2x = \cos^2 x - \sin^2 x</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>\cosh^2 x = \dfrac{\cosh 2x + 1}{2}</m></cell>
<cell><m>\cos^2 x = \dfrac{1 + \cos 2x}{2}</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>\sinh^2 x = \dfrac{\cosh 2x - 1}{2}</m></cell>
<cell><m>\sin^2 x = \dfrac{1 - \cos 2x}{2}</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</table>

<p>
Notice the pattern: each hyperbolic identity is its trigonometric
counterpart with the sign changed wherever two sines (or two tangents)
are multiplied together.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-skel-hyp-arccosh">
<title>Computing <m>\cosh^{-1}(x)</m></title>
<statement>
<p>
Show that the inverse hyperbolic cosine can be written in terms of the
natural logarithm as
<md>
\cosh^{-1}(x) = \ln\left(x + \sqrt{x^2-1}\right), \qquad x \geq 1.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Restrict <m>\cosh</m> to <m>x \geq 0</m> so that it is one-to-one, set
<m>y = \cosh(x)</m>, and solve for <m>x</m>.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,15);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,14.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for a handwritten solution.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-skel-hyp-integral">
<title>An integral via a hyperbolic substitution</title>
<statement>
<p>
Use hyperbolic functions to calculate the integral
<md>
\int \frac{\sqrt{x^2-1}}{x^2}\,dx, \qquad x \ge 1.
</md>
<em>Hint:</em> Similar to <m>\int \sec^2(x)\,dx = \tan(x) + C</m>, we
have <m>\int \operatorname{sech}^2(x)\,dx = \tanh(x) + C</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,15);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,14.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for a handwritten solution.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>
</section>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<section xml:id="sec-skel-hyp-real-life">
<title>Hyperbolic Functions in Real Life</title>

<introduction>
<p>
Hyperbolic functions are more than a formal analogue of the
trigonometric functions: they describe a number of concrete physical
situations. We look at three of them.
</p>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-hyp-catenary">
<title>*Hanging Cables and the Catenary</title>

<p>
<em>Starred section.</em> This one is for the interested reader. It will not
be examined.
</p>

<p>
If a heavy flexible cable (such as a telephone line, a power line, or a
chain) hangs freely from two supports, it settles into a curve called a
<term>catenary</term>. Contrary to a common guess, this curve is not a
parabola; it is the graph of a hyperbolic cosine,
<md>
y = a\cosh\left(\frac{x}{a}\right),
</md>
where <m>x</m> is measured horizontally from the lowest point of the
cable, so that the <m>y</m>-axis is the axis of symmetry and the lowest
point sits at height <m>y(0) = a</m>. Sliding the curve up or down, as
in <m>y = c + a\cosh(x/a)</m>, only changes where we draw the
<m>x</m>-axis.
</p>

<p>
The shape comes out of a balance of forces. Look at the piece of cable
running from the lowest point to a point where the cable makes an angle
<m>\theta</m> with the horizontal, and let <m>s</m> be the arc length of
that piece. Three forces act on it: the tension <m>T_0</m> at the lowest
point, which is horizontal; the tension <m>T</m> along the cable at the
other end; and the weight <m>\lambda g s</m> of the piece, where
<m>\lambda</m> is the mass per unit length; see
<xref ref="fig-skel-hyp-catenary-forces"/>. Balancing the horizontal and the
vertical components gives
<md>
<mrow>T\cos\theta \amp = T_0</mrow>
<mrow>T\sin\theta \amp = \lambda g s.</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-hyp-catenary-forces">
<caption>
The three forces on the piece of cable of arc length <m>s</m> running
from the lowest point of the cable to a point where the cable makes an
angle <m>\theta</m> with the horizontal. The lowest point sits at height
<m>a = T_0/(\lambda g)</m>, and the supports are a distance <m>2b</m>
apart. (After Fig. 1 of Behroozi, cited below.)
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-hyp-catenary-forces">
<diagram dimensions="(400,300)" margins="12">
<definition>c(x) = (exp(x) + exp(-x))/2</definition>
<definition>P = (1.1, 1.6685)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.5,-0.5,2.9,3.3)">
<axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no"/>
<graph at="cf-curve" function="c" domain="(-1.75,1.75)"
       stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
<graph at="cf-arc" function="c" domain="(0,1.1)"
       stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="4"/>
<line at="cf-horiz" endpoints="(P,(1.85,1.6685))" stroke="gray" thickness="1"/>
<angle-marker at="cf-theta" points="((1.85,1.6685),P,(1.4,2.0685))" radius="24"
              clear-background="no">
<m>\theta</m>
</angle-marker>
<vector at="cf-T" v="(0.36,0.48)" tail="P" stroke="black" thickness="2"
        alignment="ne"><m>T</m></vector>
<vector at="cf-W" v="(0,-0.6)" tail="P" stroke="black" thickness="2"
        alignment="e"><m>\lambda g s</m></vector>
<vector at="cf-T0" v="(-0.6,0)" tail="(0,1)" stroke="black" thickness="2"
        alignment="nw"><m>T_0</m></vector>
<point at="cf-vertex" p="(0,1)" fill="black"/>
<point at="cf-P" p="P" fill="black"/>
<line at="cf-a" endpoints="((0,0),(0,1))" stroke="gray" thickness="2"
      arrows="2"/>
<label at="cf-a-lab" anchor="(-0.06,0.5)" alignment="w"><m>a</m></label>
<label at="cf-s-lab" anchor="(0.62,1.02)" alignment="se" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>s</m>
</label>
<line at="cf-b" endpoints="((0,3.05),(1.75,3.05))" stroke="gray" thickness="1"
      arrows="2"/>
<label at="cf-b-lab" anchor="(0.87,3.08)" alignment="n"><m>b</m></label>
<line at="cf-support" endpoints="((1.75,2.925),(1.75,3.15))" stroke="gray"
      thickness="1"/>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A hanging cable in the shape of a catenary. The piece of the cable from its lowest point up to a point P is drawn in dark red and labelled s. Three force arrows are shown: the horizontal tension T naught at the lowest point, the tension T along the cable at P, and the weight lambda g s pointing straight down at P. The angle theta is marked between the cable at P and the horizontal. The height of the lowest point above the x axis is labelled a, and the horizontal distance from the y axis to the support is labelled b.">
  <annotation ref="cf-curve" text="The catenary y equals a hyperbolic cosine of x over a."/>
  <annotation ref="cf-arc" text="The section of cable of arc length s, from the vertex to the point P."/>
  <annotation ref="cf-T0" text="The horizontal tension T naught at the lowest point of the cable."/>
  <annotation ref="cf-T" text="The tension T at P, directed along the cable."/>
  <annotation ref="cf-W" text="The weight lambda g s of the section, pointing straight down."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The quantity <m>a = T_0/(\lambda g)</m> has units of length, and
dividing the second equation by the first eliminates <m>T</m> and leaves
<m>s = a\tan\theta</m>. Since the cable makes the angle <m>\theta</m>
with the horizontal, <m>\tan\theta</m> is its slope, so
<md>
\frac{dy}{dx} = \tan\theta = \frac{s}{a}.
</md>
Now use the arc length element
<m>ds = \sqrt{(dx)^2 + (dy)^2} = \sqrt{1 + (s/a)^2}\,dx</m> and separate
the variables:
<md>
dx = \frac{a\,ds}{\sqrt{a^2+s^2}}
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
x = a\sinh^{-1}\!\left(\frac{s}{a}\right),
</md>
where the constant of integration vanishes because <m>s = 0</m> at
<m>x = 0</m>.
</p>

<p>
Solving for <m>s</m> gives <m>s = a\sinh(x/a)</m>, and therefore
<md>
\frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{s}{a} = \sinh\left(\frac{x}{a}\right).
</md>
One last integration, together with <m>y(0) = a</m>, produces the
catenary equation <m>y = a\cosh(x/a)</m>.
</p>

<p>
Two things are worth noticing. First, <m>a</m> is a pure scale factor:
written as <m>y/a = \cosh(x/a)</m>, the equation shows that every
catenary is a scaled copy of the single curve <m>y = \cosh x</m>, in
exactly the same way that every circle is a scaled copy of the unit
circle; <xref ref="fig-skel-hyp-catenary-family"/> shows four of them.
Second, <m>a</m> is fixed by the cable itself. If the cable has
half-length <m>l</m> and its two supports are a distance <m>2b</m>
apart, then putting <m>s = l</m> at <m>x = b</m> in
<m>s = a\sinh(x/a)</m> gives
<md>
l = a\sinh\left(\frac{b}{a}\right),
</md>
which determines <m>a</m> (numerically) from the two lengths. Since
<m>a = T_0/(\lambda g)</m>, a cable pulled tight has a large <m>a</m>
and hangs almost flat, while a slack one has a small <m>a</m> and sags
sharply.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-hyp-catenary-family">
<caption>
The catenaries <m>y = a\cosh(x/a)</m> for
<m>a = 0.5,\ 1,\ 2,\ 4</m>. Each curve meets the <m>y</m>-axis at its
own value of <m>a</m>, and all four are scaled copies of the single
curve <m>y = \cosh x</m>. (After Fig. 2 of Behroozi, cited below.)
</caption>
<image width="72%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-skel-hyp-catenary-family">
<diagram dimensions="(420,320)" margins="12">
<definition>ca(x) = 0.5*(exp(x/0.5) + exp(-x/0.5))/2</definition>
<definition>cb(x) = (exp(x) + exp(-x))/2</definition>
<definition>cc(x) = 2*(exp(x/2) + exp(-x/2))/2</definition>
<definition>cd(x) = 4*(exp(x/4) + exp(-x/4))/2</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-6.9,-1.1,6.9,9.6)">
<axes at="cfam-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" hlabels="(-6,2,6)" vlabels="(0,2,8)"/>
<graph at="cfam-a" function="ca" domain="(-1.6,1.6)"
       stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="2"/>
<graph at="cfam-b" function="cb" domain="(-2.6,2.6)"
       stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="3"/>
<graph at="cfam-c" function="cc" domain="(-3.9,3.9)"
       stroke="#E69F00" thickness="2"/>
<graph at="cfam-d" function="cd" domain="(-5.6,5.6)"
       stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
<point at="cfam-ia" p="(0,0.5)" fill="#8B1A4A"/>
<point at="cfam-ib" p="(0,1)" fill="#56B4E9"/>
<point at="cfam-ic" p="(0,2)" fill="#E69F00"/>
<point at="cfam-id" p="(0,4)" fill="#009E73"/>
<label at="cfam-la" anchor="(1.6,6.28)" alignment="n" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>a=0.5</m>
</label>
<label at="cfam-lb" anchor="(-2.6,6.9)" alignment="n" color="#56B4E9">
<m>a=1</m>
</label>
<label at="cfam-lc" anchor="(3.9,7.19)" alignment="n" color="#E69F00">
<m>a=2</m>
</label>
<label at="cfam-ld" anchor="(5.6,8.73)" alignment="n" color="#009E73">
<m>a=4</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Four catenaries y equals a hyperbolic cosine of x over a, drawn on the same axes for a equal to one half, one, two, and four. Each curve is a U shape whose lowest point sits on the y axis at height a; the larger the value of a, the wider and flatter the curve.">
  <annotation ref="cfam-a" text="The catenary with scale factor one half, the narrowest of the four."/>
  <annotation ref="cfam-b" text="The unit catenary y equals hyperbolic cosine of x, with lowest point at height one."/>
  <annotation ref="cfam-c" text="The catenary with scale factor two."/>
  <annotation ref="cfam-d" text="The catenary with scale factor four, the widest and flattest of the four."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
A worked example of a hanging cable, in which we find the slope of the
cable and the angle at which it meets its pole, appears in
<xref ref="example-skel-hyp-catenary"/>.
</p>

<p>
The derivation above follows F. Behroozi,
<url href="https://pubs.aip.org/aapt/pte/article/56/4/214/318980/In-Praise-of-the-Catenary" visual="pubs.aip.org">In Praise of the Catenary</url>,
<em>The Physics Teacher</em> <term>56</term>, 214<ndash/>217 (2018),
which also discusses the sense in which all catenaries are similar to
one another and suggests simple classroom demonstrations.
</p>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-hyp-celestial">
<title>Celestial Mechanics</title>

<p>
If a comet has enough speed, it can escape the gravitational pull of the
sun, in which case one possible trajectory is a hyperbolic trajectory.
The comet
<url href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/2i-borisov/" visual="science.nasa.gov">2I/Borisov</url>,
discovered in 2019, is the first comet known to have come from outside
our solar system. It was moving too fast for the sun to capture it, so
its path is a hyperbola rather than an ellipse: it swung around the sun
once and is now on its way back out. See
<xref ref="fig-skel-hyp-comet-borisov"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-hyp-comet-borisov">
<caption>
The interstellar comet 2I/Borisov, photographed by the Hubble Space
Telescope in 2019. Because its speed exceeds the escape speed of the
sun, its trajectory is a hyperbola and it passes through the solar
system only once. (Image: NASA, ESA and D. Jewitt (UCLA).)
</caption>
<image source="app-hyperbola-comet-borisov.jpg" width="60%">
<shortdescription>
A fuzzy blue comet with a bright core and a broad tail sweeping to the
upper right, against a black background.
</shortdescription>
</image>
</figure>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-hyp-gateway-arch">
<title>The Gateway Arch</title>

<p>
<term>The Gateway Arch in St. Louis, Missouri</term> (designed in 1963
and completed in 1965) is a catenary turned upside down: flipping the
curve converts the tension carried by a hanging chain into pure
compression, which is what masonry and steel carry best. The geometric
form of the gateway was set by Hannskari Bandel (structural engineer)
and was expressed in the blueprints by the equation
<md>
y = A\left(\cosh\frac{Cx}{L} - 1\right),
</md>
where <m>A</m>, <m>C</m>, and <m>L</m> are constants. The arch is
slightly flattened compared with a uniform hanging chain, because it is
thicker at the base than at the top. It stands 630 feet tall and 630
feet wide at the base; the National Park Service describes its
construction and its geometry at
<url href="https://www.nps.gov/jeff/index.htm" visual="nps.gov">Gateway Arch National Park</url>.
See <xref ref="fig-skel-hyp-gateway-arch"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-hyp-gateway-arch">
<caption>
The Gateway Arch in St. Louis, Missouri. Its centerline follows the
curve <m>y = A\left(\cosh(Cx/L) - 1\right)</m>, an upside-down catenary.
(Photograph by John Margolies, 1988; John Margolies Roadside America
photograph archive, Library of Congress, Prints and Photographs
Division.)
</caption>
<image source="app-catenary-gateway-arch.jpg" width="48%">
<shortdescription>
The stainless steel Gateway Arch rising from a line of trees against a
clear blue sky, curving up to a rounded peak and back down.
</shortdescription>
</image>
</figure>
</subsection>
</section>

